---
title: Site
summary: A documentation-site layer on top of collections that arranges them into a navigable site with a nav tree, prev/next, search, a sitemap and a shell. Its read functions are safe to call from the browser while the corpus stays on the server.
related:
  - content/outline
  - content/shell
  - content/collections
---

A [collection](/docs/content/collections) answers the question of **what exists**: a set of typed entries. Once you have more than a handful of pages, you also need to decide **how they read as a site**, which means a navigation tree, an order, prev/next links, a search index, a sitemap and a shell around each page. The site layer handles that. It builds directly on collections and lives in the same `@ogygia/content` package. This docs site is built with it, so every page you read here is arranged, navigated and searched by it. The **[Playground ↗](/playground)** is a second example, with the stock shell, nine swappable themes, a version × locale switcher, a blog genre and an OpenAPI reference, all created by a single `site()` call.

You give `site()` your collections and an arrangement, and it creates small, browser-safe functions that return data for your routes and the shell to use. The corpus itself never crosses the wire. If all you need is a list and a body on one page, `content()` on its own is enough; reach for `site()` when you want a sidebar, "keep reading" links, a search box, `llms.txt`, or versioned or localized docs.

## From zero

You can scaffold the whole thing (the site module, a content folder, routes and a `DocsShell` layout) with one command:

::: code-group

```bash [npm]
npx ogygia site init
```

```bash [pnpm]
pnpm dlx ogygia site init
```

:::

The scaffold is ordinary app code that you own: a `folder()` collection, a `site()` next to it, a catch-all route and a layout that mounts the shell. The rest of this page explains what those few files do.

## The whole thing in one file

`site()` takes a single options object, in which `outline` is the only required key, and returns a `Site`. It lives in a **`.server.ts` module** next to the collections it arranges:

```ts title=src/lib/site.server.ts
// server-only: the corpus can never reach a client bundle
import { site, links } from '@ogygia/content';
import { guides } from './collections.server';

export const docs = site({
	outline: guides,        // a bare collection arranges itself by convention (see below)
	prevNext: 'graph',      // "keep reading" follows the content graph
	checks: [links()],      // a broken in-prose link fails the build
	data: {
		title: 'Example',
		description: 'Docs for Example.',
		origin: 'https://example.dev'   // absolute URLs for sitemap/llms when prerendered
	}
});
```

That is the complete configuration for this site. `docs` is a `folder()` collection, so its `NN-` filename prefixes already encode the order and its `+meta.json` sidecars name the sections. The outline reads that structure as **data** and builds the tree without any further configuration. When you want to arrange things by hand, you can point it at several collections, a `pick()` selection or explicit groups, as described under [outline](/docs/content/outline).

::: warning Collections belong in a server module
A collection's loader is an eager glob of the whole compiled corpus, so define it in a `.server.ts` / `.remote.ts` file (or under `src/lib/server/`), where Kit keeps it out of client bundles. ogygia **warns** you at build time if you don't; [Content collections](/docs/content/collections) explains why.
:::

## What site() returns

The site you create (name it after what it is, such as `docs` or `blog`) is a set of read functions. Each one returns plain data, and none of them pulls the corpus into the browser:

| function              | returns                                                          | used by                      |
| --------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------- |
| `docs.nav()`          | the sidebar tree, hrefs resolved                                 | the shell / a nav remote     |
| `docs.meta()`         | `{ nav, switcher, data }` in one call                            | `<DocsShell {meta}>`         |
| `docs.page(slug)`     | the view of one page: data, headings, body, prev/next            | the page component           |
| `docs.search(q)`      | ranked hits                                                      | a search remote / the worker |
| `docs.switcher(slug)` | the version/locale picker (dimensioned sites)                    | `<Switcher>`                 |
| `docs.emit.*`         | GET handlers: sitemap, llms.txt, RSS, raw markdown, search index | `+server.ts` routes          |
| `docs.check()`        | check findings, as data                                          | vitest / CI                  |
| `docs.entries()`      | each leaf slug + declared old addresses                          | prerender `entries`          |
| `docs.load`           | the 404 guard + alias 308s + per-page checks                     | the route's `load`           |

Because they return data, **where** they run is up to you. You can prerender the nav or fetch it per request, and query search on the server or ship the static index to a client worker. The [remotes](/docs/content/remotes) helper creates the common wire surface for you.

## The wire layer

The collection holds the compiled corpus, which may be megabytes of markdown, and the site layer never serializes it. A page's **body** crosses the wire as a [held region](/docs/regions/held-regions) ticket (baked HTML rather than source), and everything else is metadata. [`remotes()`](/docs/content/remotes) creates the crossing from `site` in a server-only `.remote.ts`, so the corpus stays on the server and the browser receives only the ticketed data:

```ts title=src/lib/site.remote.ts
// the wire layer
import { remotes } from '@ogygia/content/server';
import { docs } from './site.server';

export const { nav, meta, page, search } = remotes(docs, { base: '/docs' });
```

## Render it

The page component calls one function and renders the resulting view. The [`<Doc>`](/docs/content/shell) component lays out the article, the on-this-page rail and prev/next from a `docs.page()` view, and the [shell](/docs/content/shell) wraps it with the sidebar, the header and search:

```svelte title=src/routes/docs/[...slug]/+page.svelte
<script>
	import { page } from '$app/state';
	import { Doc } from '@ogygia/content';
	import * as docs from '$lib/site.remote';

	const view = await docs.page(page.params.slug);
</script>

<Doc {view} />
```

The layout mounts the shell over the `meta` remote, so the corpus never enters the layout's module graph:

```svelte title=src/routes/docs/+layout.svelte
<script>
	import DocsShell from '@ogygia/content/docs-shell';
	import '@ogygia/content/theme.css';
	import '@ogygia/content/shell.css';
	import { meta } from '$lib/site.remote';
	let { children } = $props();

	const shellMeta = await meta();
</script>

<DocsShell meta={shellMeta} base="/docs" title="Docs">
	{@render children()}
</DocsShell>
```

## Request context: previews and roles

Each read function takes an optional **read context**, a plain object that your `context` function derives from the request. Collections pass it into their `filter`, so one site can serve a public view and a preview view from the same corpus:

```ts
export const docs = site({
	outline: guides,
	context: ({ cookies }) => ({ preview: cookies?.get?.('preview') === '1' })
});

// a draft page exists only in the preview projection
const publicView = await docs.page('roadmap');                        // null — filtered out
const draftView = await docs.page('roadmap', { context: { preview: true } });
```

The empty context `{}` is the public, prerendered view. A site gated by context usually switches its [remotes](/docs/content/remotes) to `query` mode, so that each request derives its own context.

::: details The full options bag
Every `site()` key at a glance; `outline` is the only required one.

| key          | what it does                                                                           |
| ------------ | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `outline`    | a collection, an `outline([...])` spec, or `dimensions()`: the arrangement             |
| `data`       | `{ title, description, origin }`: site facts; emissions default from here              |
| `base`       | default mount prefix for each read; a per-call `{ base }` overrides                    |
| `prevNext`   | `'order'` (default) or `'graph'`: how "keep reading" is chosen                         |
| `trail`      | `'site'` (default) or `'group'`: whether prev/next crosses section boundaries          |
| `checks`     | `[links(), ...yours]`: corpus invariants; see [Checks](/docs/content/checks)           |
| `components` | markdown element overrides, as real component values; see [Shell](/docs/content/shell) |
| `context`    | derive `{ preview, roles, … }` from the request; threads into collection filters       |
| `search`     | `{ engine }`: swap the search engine; see [Search](/docs/content/search)               |
| `redirects`  | old addresses per entry (the default reads `data.redirect_from`): baked 308 stubs      |
:::

## The map

Each part of the site layer has its own page:

- **[Outline](/docs/content/outline)** arranges collections into a tree, and covers the spec grammar, filename conventions, the address map and prev/next.
- **[Dimensions](/docs/content/dimensions)** runs one site across a matrix of versions or locales, with a switcher and graceful fallback.
- **[Shell & components](/docs/content/shell)** covers `Frame` (headless), `DocsShell` and `BlogShell` (complete shells), plus `Doc`, `Sidebar`, `Pager` and the theme.
- **[Remotes](/docs/content/remotes)** is the wire layer: `nav`, `meta`, `page` and `search`, prerendered or live.
- **[Search](/docs/content/search)** covers the search function, the static index, the on-device worker and the no-JS fallback.
- **[Emissions](/docs/content/emit)** are the machine-facing outputs: sitemap, `llms.txt`, RSS and raw markdown.
- **[Checks](/docs/content/checks)** are the link audit and custom checks that fail the build instead of the visitor.

## Do / don't

- **Do** keep `site()` and its collections in a server module and create the wire crossing in a `.remote.ts`. That split is what keeps the corpus out of client bundles.
- **Do** let filename conventions carry the structure (`NN-` order, `+meta.json` labels). The outline reads them as data, so the common case needs no arrangement config.
- **Don't** pass a collection's bodies over the wire yourself. Let `docs.page()` bake the body into a region ticket, which is the only form meant to cross.
- **Don't** use the site layer to render one standalone page; that is `content()` plus `<Region>`. The site layer is the _site_ around many pages.
